<body>
    <h1>Object Detection</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <h2 id=3D"ObjectDetection-Introduction"><strong>Introduction</stron=
g></h2>
<p>Object detection is one of the most exciting areas of artificial intelli=
gence, allowing systems to identify and locate specific objects in images. =
When it comes to application development, using frameworks like <em>Core ML=
</em> and <em>Vision</em> in Swift makes this task more accessible. These f=
rameworks offer tools for implementing computer vision solutions, making it=
 easier to integrate trained machine learning models directly into iOS devi=
ces.</p>
<h3 id=3D"ObjectDetection-WhatisObjectDetection?">What is Object Detection?=
</h3>
<p>Object detection is a computer vision task that involves identifying obj=
ects in images or videos.</p>
<p>It analyzes images to extract characteristics like color, shape, and tex=
ture, and then uses machine learning to classify the objects.</p>
<h3 id=3D"ObjectDetection-StepsinObjectDetection">Steps in Object Detection=
</h3>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Image acquisition:</strong> Using a camera or another imaging de=
vice to capture images or videos.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Classification:</strong> Using machine learning algorithms to cl=
assify the objects.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Post-processing:</strong> Refining the results, eliminating fals=
e positives, and improving accuracy.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>A lot of the progress in object detection tech is a result of the evolut=
ion of deep learning algorithms, which are specialized forms of machine lea=
rning based on learning information at multiple stages.</p>
<p>While traditional machine learning algorithms improve their performance =
with more available data, deep learning improves through layered processing=
, making it more scalable for both supervised and unsupervised learning.</p=
>
<p>For our example, we=E2=80=99ll use a pre-trained model to make it easier=
 to demonstrate and replicate. You can get the model from Apple=E2=80=99s w=
ebsite: <a class=3D"external-link" data-card-appearance=3D"inline" href=3D"=
https://developer.apple.com/machine-learning/models/" rel=3D"nofollow">http=
s://developer.apple.com/machine-learning/models/</a></p>
<h3 id=3D"ObjectDetection-ImplementinganObjectDetector">Implementing an Obj=
ect Detector</h3>
<p>To implement an object detector, the first step is asking for camera per=
mission, so we can use it with our detector.</p>
<p>To allow the app to use the camera, it=E2=80=99s necessary to add a priv=
acy setting in the application's target configuration.</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>In Xcode, click on the application.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Next, click on the target and then go to Info.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Then, in the last option, add the field "Privacy - Camera Usage Descript=
ion=E2=80=9D and next to it, write the message that=E2=80=99ll be shown whe=
n asking for permission.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>To get started with the implementation, we=E2=80=99ll create a detector =
and import our pre-trained model:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func set=
upDetector() {
    let modelURL =3D Bundle.main.url(forResource: "YOLOv3Int8LUT", withExte=
nsion: "mlmodelc")

    do {
        let visionModel =3D try VNCoreMLModel(for: MLModel(contentsOf: mode=
lURL!))
        let recognitions =3D VNCoreMLRequest(model: visionModel, completion=
Handler: detectionDidComplete)
        self.requests =3D [recognitions]
    } catch let error {
        print(error)
    }
}

func detectionDidComplete(request: VNRequest, error: Error?) {
    DispatchQueue.main.async(execute: {
        if let results =3D request.results {
            self.extractDetections(results)
        }
    })
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>With it, we=E2=80=99ll extract the detections captured through the camer=
a and create boxes to represent the detected objects:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func ext=
ractDetections(_ results: [VNObservation]) {
    detectionLayer.sublayers =3D nil
   =20
    for observation in results where observation is VNRecognizedObjectObser=
vation {
        guard let objectObservation =3D observation as? VNRecognizedObjectO=
bservation else { continue }
       =20
        let objectBounds =3D VNImageRectForNormalizedRect(objectObservation=
.boundingBox, Int(screenRect.size.width), Int(screenRect.size.height))
        let transformedBounds =3D CGRect(x: objectBounds.minX, y: screenRec=
t.size.height - objectBounds.maxY, width: objectBounds.maxX - objectBounds.=
minX, height: objectBounds.maxY - objectBounds.minY)
       =20
        let boxLayer =3D self.drawBoundingBox(transformedBounds)

        detectionLayer.addSublayer(boxLayer)
    }
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func set=
upLayers() {
    detectionLayer =3D CALayer()
    detectionLayer.frame =3D CGRect(x: 0, y: 0, width: screenRect.size.widt=
h, height: screenRect.size.height)
    self.view.layer.addSublayer(detectionLayer)
}
   =20
func updateLayers() {
    detectionLayer?.frame =3D CGRect(x: 0, y: 0, width: screenRect.size.wid=
th, height: screenRect.size.height)
}
   =20
func drawBoundingBox(_ bounds: CGRect) -&gt; CALayer {
    let boxLayer =3D CALayer()
    boxLayer.frame =3D bounds
    boxLayer.borderWidth =3D 3.0
    boxLayer.borderColor =3D CGColor.init(red: 0.0, green: 0.0, blue: 0.0, =
alpha: 1.0)
    boxLayer.cornerRadius =3D 4
    return boxLayer
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>After that, all that=E2=80=99s left is to display the results in a view.=
 Since we=E2=80=99re adding sublayers based on what=E2=80=99s detected, wit=
h every new input during display, the view gets updated with the borders of=
 the new detections using the following function:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func set=
upCaptureSession() {
    // Camera input
    guard let videoDevice =3D AVCaptureDevice.default(.builtInDualWideCamer=
a,for: .video, position: .back) else { return }
    guard let videoDeviceInput =3D try? AVCaptureDeviceInput(device: videoD=
evice) else { return }
     =20
    guard captureSession.canAddInput(videoDeviceInput) else { return }
    captureSession.addInput(videoDeviceInput)
                   =20
    // Preview layer
    screenRect =3D UIScreen.main.bounds
   =20
    previewLayer =3D AVCaptureVideoPreviewLayer(session: captureSession)
    previewLayer.frame =3D CGRect(x: 0, y: 0, width: screenRect.size.width,=
 height: screenRect.size.height)
    previewLayer.videoGravity =3D AVLayerVideoGravity.resizeAspectFill // F=
ill screen
    previewLayer.connection?.videoRotationAngle =3D 90
   =20
    // Detector
    videoOutput.setSampleBufferDelegate(self, queue: DispatchQueue(label: "=
sampleBufferQueue"))
    captureSession.addOutput(videoOutput)
   =20
    videoOutput.connection(with: .video)?.videoRotationAngle =3D 90
   =20
    // Updates to UI must be on main queue
    DispatchQueue.main.async { [weak self] in
        self!.view.layer.addSublayer(self!.previewLayer)
    }
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"ObjectDetection-Conclusion"><strong>Conclusion</strong></h2>
<p>Using Swift along with the <em>Core ML</em> and <em>Vision</em> framewor=
ks allows developers to build smart, fast apps that take advantage of machi=
ne learning directly on iOS devices. With these technologies constantly evo=
lving, the future of object detection in mobile apps looks bright, opening =
up more and more opportunities for innovation across different areas.</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
